Understood — you want a true fine-grained reactivity core (Angular-style `signal` / `computed` / `effect`), not a "re-render + diff" shortcut. That's a better model and aligns with the project's own roadmap (AGENTS.md lists "computed/derived signals + effects" as the planned next step). Here's the revised reactive design, then a couple of tradeoff questions.

## Revised reactive subsystem (`com.pathland.dsl.signal`)

Mirrors Angular's semantics exactly, implemented as a zero-dependency Java 22+ module:

```java
public interface Signal<T> { T get(); }

public interface WritableSignal<T> extends Signal<T> {
    void set(T value);
    void update(UnaryOperator<T> fn);
    Signal<T> asReadonly();
}

public final class Signals {
    public static <T> WritableSignal<T> signal(T initial);                 // equality = Objects::equals
    public static <T> WritableSignal<T> signal(T initial, BiPredicate<T,T> equal);
    public static <T> Signal<T> computed(Supplier<T> fn);
    public static <T> Signal<T> computed(Supplier<T> fn, BiPredicate<T,T> equal);
    public static EffectRef effect(Runnable fn);
    public static EffectRef effect(Runnable fn, EffectOptions opts);       // allowSignalWrites, manualCleanup
    public static <T> T untracked(Supplier<T> fn);
}
public final class EffectOptions { boolean allowSignalWrites; boolean manualCleanup; }
public interface EffectRef { void destroy(); }
```

**Semantics (Angular parity, each covered by a ported unit test):**
- **Lazy + memoized `computed`** — fn runs only on first read; cached until a producer changes; result reused on unchanged dependencies.
- **Glitch-free** — diamond graph (`b = computed(a)`, `c = computed(a)`, `d = computed(b,c)`) recomputes `d` exactly once per `a` change; effects flush after the synchronous write settles, never mid-graph.
- **Equality suppression** — a `set` that yields an equal value does not propagate.
- **Error caching** — a computed that throws caches the throwable and rethrows on read until a producer changes (Angular behavior); no infinite loops.
- **Write discipline** — writes inside `computed`/`effect` are forbidden by default (`allowSignalWrites: false`), preventing cycles; `allowSignalWrites: true` opts out.
- **`untracked`** — reads that don't register a dependency (needed for event handlers / loggers).
- **Circular-dependency detection** — guards against `a → b → a`.
- **Dependency tracking** via a `ThreadLocal` reactive-context stack (single-threaded app actor, matching the `spawn_local` model in `examples/pathland-web`); a pluggable `Scheduler` drives effect flushing.

## Emitter integration (fine-grained partial emit)

Replaces the "re-render + diff" idea. The retained tree records **node bindings**, not a full rediff:

- `Text` accepts a `String` **or** a `Signal<String>`; reactive modifiers accept `Signal<Float>`/`Signal<Color>` (e.g. `.fontSize(Signal<Float>)`, `.color(Signal<Color>)`).
- `Emitter` registers a per-binding **effect**: on `signal.set(...)`, only that binding's node re-emits `STYLE::SET_TEXT` / `STYLE::SET_PROPERTY` — identical to Rust's `set_signal` partial-emit contract. An unchanged signal emits zero opcodes.
- `Binding<T>` (§2E) becomes a thin signal-backed convenience on top of `WritableSignal<T>`, keeping the `TextField(placeholder, Binding<String>)` and `StateStore` APIs intact while `Signal` is the true primitive.
- Effects also drive cross-cutting concerns: e.g. an effect persisting `counter` to `StateStore`, or flushing a frame to the WebSocket sink.

Rust's role shrinks accordingly: `libpathland_core` remains a passive SPSC ring (`pathland_ring_buffer_push`), and reactive state/diffing lives entirely in Java (no `pathland_native_signal_*`).

A few tradeoffs before I finalize:

